<h2>The route in Delhi: Family Court to Division Bench</h2>
<p>Since matrimonial jurisdiction in Delhi vests in the Family Courts, the operative appellate provision is Section 19 of the Family Courts Act: an appeal lies to the High Court from every judgment or order (not interlocutory) of a Family Court, and it is heard by a Bench of two Judges. These are the MAT.APP.(F.C.) matters listed before the Division Benches of the Delhi High Court. Section 28 HMA supplies the substantive appealability rules — what counts as an appealable decree or order — while the Family Courts Act supplies the forum and its own limitation. An appeal challenging only quantum of costs is barred; challenges to alimony and custody orders made in the decree travel with the main appeal.</p>

<h2>What a matrimonial first appeal involves</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The record is everything.</strong> The appeal is decided on the trial record — pleadings, issues, depositions, exhibited documents. Fresh evidence enters only through the narrow gate of Order XLI Rule 27 CPC, on grounds such as due diligence or the appellate court&rsquo;s own requirement.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Grounds that work.</strong> Misapplication of the legal standard for cruelty or desertion; findings resting on unpleaded allegations; reliance on evidence never put to the witness; decrees granted despite condonation of the alleged conduct; and procedural denials, such as refusal of a genuine opportunity to cross-examine.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Interim protection.</strong> The appellate court can stay operation of the decree and regulate interim alimony and custody during the appeal. A respondent-spouse dependent on the decree-holder should seek interim directions at admission.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Settlement remains open.</strong> Division Benches routinely refer matrimonial appeals to mediation; a settlement can convert the contest into a mutual-consent dissolution with agreed terms on alimony, children and pending cases.</div>
</div>

<h2>Timelines and the remarriage trap</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Provision</th><th>Period</th><th>Note</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 19(3), Family Courts Act</td><td>30 days from the judgment</td><td>The forum-specific period for Family Court appeals; the prudent deadline in Delhi.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 28(4), HMA</td><td>90 days from the decree</td><td>The HMA&rsquo;s own period after the 2003 amendment; do not rely on it over the 30-day period without advice.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 15, HMA</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Remarriage lawful only once the right of appeal is exhausted — expiry without appeal, or dismissal of the appeal.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Condonation of delay is available on sufficient cause, and appellate courts in matrimonial matters do weigh the human realities of litigation fatigue; but a respondent who waits while the other side&rsquo;s position hardens — remarriage, relocation with children, disposal of assets — fights the appeal uphill regardless of condonation. Conversely, decree-holders should resist the urge to act irreversibly on a fresh decree before the appeal window closes.</p>

<h2>Beyond the Division Bench</h2>
<p>From the High Court&rsquo;s appellate judgment, the remaining remedy is a special leave petition to the Supreme Court under Article 136 — discretionary, not of right, and granted sparingly in matrimonial matters, typically where the High Court&rsquo;s approach to the legal standard or to settled rights is in question. Review before the High Court itself is confined to the narrow grounds of error apparent. The realistic appellate strategy is therefore front-loaded: the first appeal is the true second innings, and it deserves the preparation of one.</p>
<div class="note">Parallel statutes mirror this scheme — the Special Marriage Act and the Divorce Act have their own appeal provisions, and DV Act orders are appealable separately under Section 29 of that Act to the Sessions Court. This article is general information for orientation, not advice; appellate viability turns on the trial record in each case.</div>
